Q: How do I get typing to work on generic class when creating a property with a function? A: <p>The main issue is that the properties are created dynamically, so the type checker can't see that their return type is the generic <code>V</code>. It therefore falls back to <code>Any</code>.</p>
<p>If you want Pylance to correctly infer <code>custom.val1</code> as <code>V</code>, the simplest approach is to define the properties explicitly:</p>
<pre><code>@property
def val1(self) -&gt; V:
    return self._val1

@val1.setter
def val1(self, value: V) -&gt; None:
    self._val1 = value
</code></pre>
<p>It’s a little more repetitive, but static type checkers can understand it reliably. Dynamic <code>property</code> creation generally loses that type information.</p>

Q: How to use data returned by an INSERT...RETURNING in another query? A: <p>You can start a transaction, and use a temporary table C to contain the elements of B that don't exist in A. Then insert them. And perform a SELECT base on the same results. With MariaDB having a repeatable read isolation level the results won't change and the <code>FOR UPDATE</code> as part of the <code>CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE C</code> ensure they won't change during the SQL transaction.</p>
<p>The lifetime of <code>C</code> is for the connection so could exist outside the transaction if desired.</p>
<pre><code>START TRANSACTION;

CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE C AS
SELECT B.id
FROM B
LEFT JOIN A ON A.id = B.id
WHERE A.id IS NULL FOR UPDATE;

INSERT INTO A SELECT C.id...;

SELECT ... FROM C....;
DROP TEMPORARY TABLE C;
COMMIT;
</code></pre>
